WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.324
Two classic exercises close the lecture.

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.122
Each builds one structure using only

00:00:06.197 --> 00:00:08.314
the operations of the other

00:00:08.390 --> 00:00:10.734
so they test whether you really

00:00:10.809 --> 00:00:12.699
understand LIFO and FIFO.

00:00:12.699 --> 00:00:15.119
First, a stack from two queues.

00:00:15.119 --> 00:00:17.311
Keep the stack in q1

00:00:17.387 --> 00:00:19.277
with the top of the stack

00:00:19.353 --> 00:00:21.016
at the front of q1.

00:00:21.016 --> 00:00:22.150
Why the front?

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:24.872
Pop must return the newest element

00:00:24.872 --> 00:00:28.198
and a queue can only remove from its front

00:00:28.198 --> 00:00:31.525
so the newest element has to be kept there.

00:00:31.525 --> 00:00:34.020
q2 is only scratch space.

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:36.769
Push(1): q1 is empty

00:00:36.769 --> 00:00:38.976
so 1 simply goes in.

00:00:38.976 --> 00:00:39.936
Push(2)

00:00:39.936 --> 00:00:44.062
first move everything from q1 to q2.

00:00:44.062 --> 00:00:47.229
Then add 2 to the empty q1

00:00:47.229 --> 00:00:49.436
so it is at the front

00:00:49.436 --> 00:00:52.891
and move everything back behind it.

00:00:52.891 --> 00:00:54.810
Now 2 is the top.

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:56.468
Push(3).

00:00:56.468 --> 00:00:57.447
Step one

00:00:57.447 --> 00:01:01.170
move every element of q1 to q2

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:02.933
2 and then 1.

00:01:02.933 --> 00:01:07.243
Step two: add 3 to the now empty q1

00:01:07.243 --> 00:01:09.790
so 3 is at its front.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:11.261
Step three

00:01:11.261 --> 00:01:15.642
move every element of q2 back to q1

00:01:15.642 --> 00:01:17.069
behind 3.

00:01:17.069 --> 00:01:21.552
q1 now reads 3, 2, 1: top first.

00:01:21.552 --> 00:01:25.220
Six moves so far, for three pushes.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:29.463
Pushing 1 to 5 this way leaves 5

00:01:29.463 --> 00:01:32.680
4, 3, 2, 1 in q1.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:36.254
Pop is just q1.poll(): 5, 4

00:01:36.254 --> 00:01:37.773
3, 2, 1.

00:01:37.773 --> 00:01:39.560
Last in, first out.

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:41.517
Pop is O(1).

00:01:41.617 --> 00:01:44.529
But push moves the k elements

00:01:44.629 --> 00:01:47.340
already in the stack twice

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.750
so it costs Θ(k).

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:53.062
Pushing n elements costs two times

00:01:53.146 --> 00:01:55.262
0 plus 1 plus and so

00:01:55.347 --> 00:01:57.124
on up to n minus 1

00:01:57.124 --> 00:02:00.170
which is n(n - 1) moves

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:02.624
Θ(n²) in total.

00:02:02.624 --> 00:02:05.247
For five pushes, twenty moves.

00:02:05.247 --> 00:02:08.040
A real stack pushes in O(1)

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:11.509
so this is an exercise in understanding

00:02:11.509 --> 00:02:13.540
not a structure to use.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:16.680
The slide's test loop has a bug.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:18.920
It pops with i less than or

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.000
equal to keys.length

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.480
6 pops from a stack of 5.

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:29.466
The first 5 print 5, 4, 3, 2

00:02:29.466 --> 00:02:30.106
1.

00:02:30.106 --> 00:02:35.230
The sixth finds q1 empty: underflow.

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:39.075
The slide's code then prints Underflow and stops

00:02:39.148 --> 00:02:41.988
the whole program with System.exit(0).

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:43.954
Loop with i less than

00:02:44.027 --> 00:02:45.410
keys.length instead.

00:02:45.483 --> 00:02:48.760
Our version throws an IllegalStateException

00:02:48.833 --> 00:02:51.090
after printing the 5 values

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:53.420
so the bug is at least visible.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.997
The slides mention another idea

00:02:56.062 --> 00:02:57.736
that needs only one queue.

00:02:57.736 --> 00:03:00.120
Enqueue the new element at the rear.

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:03.210
Then dequeue each of the elements

00:03:03.293 --> 00:03:05.369
that were in front of it

00:03:05.369 --> 00:03:08.192
and enqueue it again at the rear.

00:03:08.192 --> 00:03:10.517
Push b: b goes in behind a

00:03:10.517 --> 00:03:12.510
then a goes round once.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:16.472
Push c: c goes in, then b and a go round.

00:03:16.472 --> 00:03:19.820
The new element ends up at the front.

00:03:19.908 --> 00:03:23.785
Rotating a queue like this is the same trick

00:03:23.873 --> 00:03:26.780
as the traffic light in part 2.

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:29.672
Pop just takes the front element

00:03:29.672 --> 00:03:30.750
it returns c.

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:33.215
The cost is the same as before

00:03:33.215 --> 00:03:34.756
push is Θ(n)

00:03:34.756 --> 00:03:36.912
with n minus 1 rotations

00:03:36.912 --> 00:03:38.530
and pop is O(1).

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:42.443
The mirror image: a queue from two stacks.

00:03:42.443 --> 00:03:44.903
Keep the queue in stack s1

00:03:44.903 --> 00:03:47.760
with the front of the queue on top.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.173
Here the queue is 1, 2: 1 is on top.

00:03:51.173 --> 00:03:54.030
Then dequeue is just s1.pop().

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:56.025
To enqueue 3

00:03:56.025 --> 00:03:59.410
it must go to the bottom of s1.

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:02.312
So pop everything onto s2

00:04:02.312 --> 00:04:04.054
1 and then 2.

00:04:04.054 --> 00:04:07.246
Push 3 onto the empty s1.

00:04:07.246 --> 00:04:11.115
And pop everything back: 2, then 1

00:04:11.115 --> 00:04:13.340
which is on top again.

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:17.430
Enqueue is Θ(n), dequeue is O(1).

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:21.257
Every enqueue moves all the elements twice

00:04:21.344 --> 00:04:23.605
so n enqueues cost n times

00:04:23.692 --> 00:04:25.258
n minus 1 moves

00:04:25.258 --> 00:04:28.737
exactly like the stack from two queues.

00:04:28.737 --> 00:04:32.216
The slides use java.util.Stack

00:04:32.216 --> 00:04:35.521
ArrayDeque has the same push and pop

00:04:35.521 --> 00:04:38.130
and is the recommended class.

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:41.512
There is a much better way

00:04:41.512 --> 00:04:44.313
a standard textbook trick.

00:04:44.313 --> 00:04:47.737
Use one stack, in, for enqueues

00:04:47.737 --> 00:04:51.057
and another, out, for dequeues.

00:04:51.057 --> 00:04:55.104
Enqueue just pushes onto in: 1, 2

00:04:55.104 --> 00:04:55.830
3.

00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:58.170
Dequeue pops from out.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:01.516
Only when out is empty does it first

00:05:01.606 --> 00:05:03.777
pour all of in into out

00:05:03.777 --> 00:05:05.676
which reverses them

00:05:05.676 --> 00:05:09.112
so the oldest element ends up on top.

00:05:09.112 --> 00:05:10.740
Then it pops out.

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:12.852
Why is this still FIFO?

00:05:12.928 --> 00:05:15.149
Every element in out is older

00:05:15.226 --> 00:05:17.141
than every element in in

00:05:17.217 --> 00:05:19.209
and out holds its elements

00:05:19.285 --> 00:05:21.047
with the oldest on top.

00:05:21.124 --> 00:05:24.187
So popping out always returns the oldest

00:05:24.264 --> 00:05:26.332
element of the whole queue

00:05:26.409 --> 00:05:29.396
and only when out runs dry do the newer

00:05:29.472 --> 00:05:31.770
elements in in get their turn.

00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:36.473
With the lazy queue: enqueue 1, 2

00:05:36.473 --> 00:05:39.729
3; dequeue; enqueue 4

00:05:39.729 --> 00:05:42.550
then dequeue 3 times.

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:45.600
What does each dequeue return

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:48.930
and which dequeues pour in into out?

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:51.900
Pause the video and work it out.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:58.255
The first dequeue finds out empty

00:05:58.255 --> 00:06:01.031
and pours 3, 2, 1 across

00:06:01.031 --> 00:06:02.092
3 moves.

00:06:02.092 --> 00:06:05.520
1 is on top of out, and it is returned.

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:08.090
Enqueue 4 goes onto in.

00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:11.848
The next 2 dequeues return 2 and 3

00:06:11.848 --> 00:06:13.470
straight from out.

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:16.857
The last dequeue finds out empty again

00:06:16.857 --> 00:06:19.427
and pours 4 across, 1 move.

00:06:19.427 --> 00:06:20.750
It returns 4.

00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:23.398
FIFO order 1, 2, 3, 4

00:06:23.398 --> 00:06:26.123
with 4 moves for 4 elements.

00:06:26.123 --> 00:06:29.627
Notice that the first dequeue was expensive

00:06:29.627 --> 00:06:32.820
3 moves, and the next 2 were free.

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:35.852
The demo checks both versions.

00:06:35.852 --> 00:06:39.447
Both are FIFO: dequeue gives 1, then 2.

00:06:39.529 --> 00:06:42.470
Then it runs 1,000 enqueues and

00:06:42.552 --> 00:06:44.840
1,000 dequeues on each.

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:48.549
The slide version moved elements

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:51.817
999,000 times

00:06:51.817 --> 00:06:53.723
n(n - 1).

00:06:53.723 --> 00:06:57.718
The lazy one moved them 1000 times

00:06:57.718 --> 00:06:58.218
n.

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:01.133
Each element is pushed onto in once

00:07:01.133 --> 00:07:04.546
moved to out at most once, and popped once.

00:07:04.623 --> 00:07:06.873
So a sequence of n operations

00:07:06.950 --> 00:07:08.967
does O(n) work in total

00:07:09.044 --> 00:07:11.449
although one particular dequeue

00:07:11.527 --> 00:07:13.311
may move many elements.

00:07:13.388 --> 00:07:15.405
The amortised cost of each

00:07:15.483 --> 00:07:17.189
operation is O(1).

00:07:17.267 --> 00:07:20.214
It is the same kind of argument as the

00:07:20.292 --> 00:07:22.696
doubling array of lecture nine

00:07:22.696 --> 00:07:25.024
an occasional expensive step

00:07:25.024 --> 00:07:27.273
paid for by many cheap ones.

00:07:27.273 --> 00:07:28.281
In practice

00:07:28.281 --> 00:07:31.772
Java's ArrayDeque already gives you a queue

00:07:31.850 --> 00:07:34.021
the point of these exercises

00:07:34.099 --> 00:07:35.340
is the analysis.

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:39.083
Let's look back over the whole lecture.

00:07:39.164 --> 00:07:41.354
A queue inserts at the rear

00:07:41.435 --> 00:07:44.273
and removes at the front: first in

00:07:44.273 --> 00:07:45.166
first out.

00:07:45.166 --> 00:07:48.166
On a linked list with head and tail

00:07:48.166 --> 00:07:50.518
every operation is O(1)

00:07:50.518 --> 00:07:53.600
A plain array queue runs out of room.

00:07:53.950 --> 00:07:56.601
The circular queue wraps around

00:07:56.687 --> 00:07:59.167
with (i + 1) mod capacity

00:07:59.253 --> 00:08:02.417
and needs a size counter or -1

00:08:02.502 --> 00:08:05.154
values to tell full from empty.

00:08:05.239 --> 00:08:07.634
A priority queue removes the

00:08:07.719 --> 00:08:09.686
highest priority first

00:08:09.772 --> 00:08:12.338
a heap will make that

00:08:12.423 --> 00:08:14.390
O(log n) in Lecture 17.

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:17.180
And a Deque works at both ends.

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:20.596
ArrayDeque is Java's stack and its queue.

00:08:20.596 --> 00:08:21.327
Finally

00:08:21.409 --> 00:08:25.068
the two exercises of this part show that LIFO

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:28.159
and FIFO can each simulate the other

00:08:28.159 --> 00:08:29.460
but at a price.

00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:31.415
And this part.

00:08:31.415 --> 00:08:35.244
One: a stack from queues rotates every

00:08:35.342 --> 00:08:38.582
element on each push, Θ(n)

00:08:38.582 --> 00:08:40.840
while pop is O(1).

00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:45.203
Two: the slides' queue from two stacks pays

00:08:45.297 --> 00:08:47.910
Θ(n) on every enqueue.

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:48.866
Three

00:08:48.967 --> 00:08:52.804
the lazy queue with an in stack and an

00:08:52.905 --> 00:08:56.339
out stack moves each element once

00:08:56.339 --> 00:09:00.580
so its operations are amortised O(1).

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:01.659
Try this.

00:09:01.659 --> 00:09:03.604
Write a method reverse

00:09:03.685 --> 00:09:06.439
which reverses a queue of integers

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:09.193
using one stack as extra storage.

00:09:09.193 --> 00:09:12.677
What does the queue 1, 2, 3, 4

00:09:12.677 --> 00:09:13.730
5 become?

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:17.720
Next time, in Lecture 11: quicksort.

